---
import CaseStudyLayout from "@layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudyHero from "@components/CaseStudyHero.astro";
import CaseStudyMeta from "@components/CaseStudyMeta.astro";
import Figure from "@components/Figure.astro";
import Pull from "@components/Pull.astro";
import ProcessColumns from "@components/ProcessColumns.astro";
import Pivot from "@components/Pivot.astro";
import Strip from "@components/Strip.astro";
import ImagePair from "@components/ImagePair.astro";
import Metrics from "@components/Metrics.astro";
import NextCaseStudy from "@components/NextCaseStudy.astro";

import ibm01 from "@img/ibm-01.webp";
import ibm02 from "@img/ibm-02.webp";
import ibm03 from "@img/ibm-03.webp";
import ibm04 from "@img/ibm-04.webp";
import ibm08 from "@img/ibm-08.webp";
import ibm09 from "@img/ibm-09.webp";
import ibm10 from "@img/ibm-10.webp";
import ibm11 from "@img/ibm-11.webp";
import ibm12 from "@img/ibm-12.webp";

const title = "Data lineage at IBM";
const description =
	"IBM's decade-old lineage tool took hours to draw a map an auditor needed instantly. Showing almost nothing, then letting people ask for more, cut load times by up to 90% and won a Red Dot Award.";
---

<CaseStudyLayout title={title} description={description}>
	<CaseStudyHero eyebrow={["Product design", "IBM", "Data lineage in watsonx"]} title="Data lineage that answers in seconds, not hours">
		When bad data reaches production at a bank, someone has to trace it back through three hundred steps
		before the auditor arrives, and IBM's decade-old tool took hours to draw the map. Every competitor's
		answer was a better way to show everything. Ours was to show almost nothing, then let people ask for
		more. The result cut load times by up to 90%, won a Red Dot Award, and is still what IBM ships six years
		later.
	</CaseStudyHero>

	<CaseStudyMeta
		items={[
			{ label: "Role", value: "Design lead, three-person UX team" },
			{ label: "Partners", value: "ING, State Street, Rabobank" },
			{ label: "Timeline", value: "9 months" },
			{ label: "Research base", value: "20+ interviews, bi-weekly co-creation workshops with three client banks" },
		]}
	/>

	<Figure
		src={ibm01}
		alt="IBM Watson Knowledge Catalog, Lineage tab for SAVINGS_ACCOUNTS. The starting asset sits in the centre with its business terms and 2 masked columns; three original-source CSV files sit on the left grouped as Client 360 and Customer 360 with counts like 2 of 24 assets; two reports and a model marked Final target sit on the right, each showing business terms and column counts."
		figNum="Fig. 01"
		lead="Start focused, stay focused."
		wide
		fit="contain"
		deck
		deckLabel="Shipped"
	>
		The lineage opens on the asset in question, its original sources on the left, its final targets on the
		right, and everything in between folded into a count like "2 of 24 assets" until someone asks. Business
		terms, masked columns, and ownership sit on the cards themselves, not behind a hover.
	</Figure>

	<section id="problem">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span><h2>The problem</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>
			Data lineage is how a bank proves where a number came from. Regulators expect it, and getting it
			wrong can cost up to $23 million in fines, so the tool that draws the map matters more than its
			modest name suggests. IBM Infosphere had been drawing that map for a decade, and it was about to be
			folded into the new watsonx platform. The brief asked for three things at once: modernize the lineage
			experience, make it fast, and put it where enterprise users already spend their day.
		</p>
		<p>
			The real problem sat underneath the brief. A modern data pipeline has more than three hundred
			possible points of failure, and Infosphere's answer was to render all of them, which meant millions
			of backend queries before a single node appeared on screen. Finding one broken step took hours and
			more than a hundred clicks. <strong class="deck-stat" data-deck-foot="IBM Infosphere, before the redesign|Up to $23M in compliance fines at stake"
				>No amount of interface polish was going to fix a diagram that took hours to load.</strong
			> The use case wasn't browsing, either. It was an emergency: bad data is in production, the compliance
			auditor is in the building, and someone has twenty minutes to find where it came from.
		</p>
		<Figure
			src={ibm02}
			alt="IBM Infosphere Information Governance Catalog, Data Lineage for EFF_DURATION: an inventory panel listing 281 assets on the left and, on the right, a dense diagram of hundreds of tiny nodes joined by grey lines, too small to read"
			figNum="Fig. 02"
			lead="Everything, all at once."
			fit="contain"
			deck
			deckLabel="Infosphere"
		>
			This was a trivial lineage by Infosphere's standards, and it still meant 281 assets in the inventory
			and every node drawn by default, each one a thumbnail too small to read. What little metadata there
			was appeared on hover, all of it technical, none of it business context.
		</Figure>
	</section>

	<section id="research">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2>What we found</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>
			Standard research would have meant interviews up front, design in a vacuum, and a usability test at
			the end. Instead I proposed recurring co-creation with three named clients, ING, State Street, and
			Rabobank, so that generating ideas and testing them happened in the same room every two weeks,
			sometimes in person at IBM's offices in Poland and more often online across three time zones. More
			than twenty interviews surfaced the questions; the workshops were where those questions got answered.
		</p>
		<p>
			What the sessions kept revealing was vigilance. Users didn't describe browsing a lineage; they
			described hunting through it, with the consequences of a miss already in motion. I turned that into a
			persona, a journey map, and a mental model diagram, and the pattern held across a retail bank and an
			asset manager alike: the frustration wasn't the size of the data, it was the absence of any clear
			signal about what was wrong and where it had started.
		</p>
		<Pull
			quote="Data lineage should be like seeing out onto the assembly line of a factory. You can see where everything is and where things are going wrong."
			attribution="ING's Chief Data Officer, during a co-creation workshop"
		/>
		<Figure
			src={ibm03}
			alt="Persona and experience map for Betty, a business analyst who consumes data. The top block lists her asks, need statements, negative emotions, pain points, and requested metadata. Below, an emotional curve runs through five phases, naive use of data, guided use, development of autonomy, continuing collaboration, and mastery, with the lowest points where she cannot answer why her data is trustworthy and realizes she depends on data producers."
			figNum="Fig. 03"
			lead="The pain was dependence, not distance."
			wide
			fit="contain"
			deck
			deckLabel="Journey map"
		>
			Betty, the business analyst who consumes the data, hits her lowest points when she can't answer why
			her data is trustworthy and realizes she has to ask the data producers. The map runs from naive use to
			mastery, and mastery, at the far right, is being able to stand between the technical and business
			sides herself.
		</Figure>
		<p>
			Desirability testing pointed the same way. Asked to choose, users responded most strongly to "show me
			the headline, then let me drill in," and least to "show me everything." <strong
				class="deck-stat"
				data-deck-label="The headline"
				data-deck-foot="What desirability testing showed|ING, State Street, Rabobank"
				>People didn't want a bigger map. They wanted the first useful answer, then the option to keep
				going.</strong
			> That preference lined up with IBM's own design system, which already leaned toward progressive disclosure,
			and with a time-to-value KPI that demanded seconds rather than hours because emergencies were the highest-value
			moment the product had.
		</p>
		<Figure
			src={ibm04}
			alt="A workshop room at IBM's offices in Poland: eight attendees stand with their backs to the camera, gathered around a wall covered in orange and blue sticky notes, while the facilitator at the wall works through them"
			figNum="Fig. 04"
			lead="Skin in the design before skin in the deal."
			tall
			fit="center"
			deck
			deckLabel="Workshop"
		>
			Concepts arrived at formal usability testing already shaped by the exact buyers IBM needed to convert.
			The same three banks went on to shape the contract renewals.
		</Figure>
	</section>

	<section id="approach">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">03</span><h2>Three sources, one view</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>
			No single reference gave us the answer. A competitor's pattern, a freshly filed patent, and a diagram
			from another IBM team each solved one part of the problem and broke on another, so none was safe to
			adopt wholesale. The idea came from naming exactly why each one fell short.
		</p>
		<ProcessColumns
			land
			deckLabel="Three sources"
			deckCaption="Each reference solved one part of the problem, performance, clarity, or an invitation to explore, and broke on the rest."
			items={[
				{
					stepLabel: "Source 01",
					frameN: "01",
					title: "The competitor",
					imgAlt: "Diagram labelled Competitor: one pink data lineage node joined by a dashed line to a grey hidden node with a single button, Show next node",
					placeholderLabel: "Collibra's one-step expansion",
					body: "Collibra let users expand a lineage one step at a time, which kept performance in check. At our scale it was tedious to the point of failure: reaching an original source through three hundred steps meant dozens of clicks, so copying it directly was ruled out.",
				},
				{
					stepLabel: "Source 02",
					frameN: "02",
					title: "The patent",
					imgAlt: "Diagram labelled Patent: four nodes in a row, the second greyed out as a hidden node, with an arrow skipping over it from the first node to the third",
					placeholderLabel: "The pending patent",
					body: "A patent filed during the project hid intermediary steps unless they'd been flagged to always show. It cut the bloat, but flagging needs metadata applied by a person or a process, an extra flow that was out of scope for an MVP and a dependency we didn't control.",
				},
				{
					stepLabel: "Source 03",
					frameN: "03",
					title: "The neighbor",
					imgAlt: "Diagram labelled Internal: two pink nodes with two grey hidden nodes between them, covered by one dark button reading Show 2 hidden nodes",
					placeholderLabel: "The DevOps team's grouped diagram",
					body: "Another IBM team was mapping automations with a diagram that grouped similar content and showed how much was inside each group. It invited exploration well, but populating those counts required the same expensive backend queries we were trying to avoid.",
				},
			]}
		/>

		<Pivot heading="Show the source and the consumer. Draw nothing else until someone asks.">
			The Summary View lands on the simplest possible level of a lineage, the first source and the end
			consumer, and puts the size and scope of everything else in the user's hands. From there a person can
			expand upstream or downstream, filter to bad quality scores or ownership tags, search for the contact
			who can file a fix, or narrow the whole diagram to a single column in a dataset. It rendered without
			manual flagging, unlike the patent; reached distant nodes without a click-by-click march, unlike
			Collibra; and avoided expensive queries on load, unlike the DevOps pattern. It won concept testing on
			both desirability and usability the first time out, and time-to-value moved from hours to seconds.
		</Pivot>

		<Strip
			full
			solo
			deckLabel="Summary View"
			figNum="Fig. 05"
			lead="Control of size and scope, handed to the user."
			items={[
				{
					frameN: "01",
					dataLabel: "Land on source and consumer",
					imgSrc: ibm08,
					imgAlt: "IBM Watson Knowledge Catalog Lineage view for SAVINGS_ACCOUNTS, with an annotation reading: assets at either end of a lineage do not change often so it's safe to cache them, assets in the middle can change by the hour which means frequent calls to the back end for updates",
				},
				{
					frameN: "02",
					dataLabel: "Expand upstream on demand",
					imgSrc: ibm09,
					imgAlt: "Annotated screen titled Lineage Navigation, subtitled you should be able to explore lineage like you were using Google Maps: hovering an asset reveals a directional arrow, clicking it opens a menu with Show next asset, Show assets to, and Show all",
				},
				{
					frameN: "03",
					dataLabel: "Filter to what matters",
					imgSrc: ibm10,
					imgAlt: "Annotated screen of the COLLECTIONS_DW node showing 2 of 14 columns filtered into view, each with its own business terms, and a note that deeper technical, operational and governance metadata sits in a toggleable info panel",
				},
			]}
		>
			Each step reveals only what was asked for. The diagram never gets bigger than the question.
		</Strip>

		<p>
			The Summary View was novel, but it didn't satisfy every requirement of a full product on its own, and
			the mandate was bigger than lineage anyway: leadership wanted lineage, the data catalog, and data
			quality folded into one experience inside watsonx. Combining them let a user see the quality of the
			data at every node in a pipeline, walk to the source of the degradation, and fix it, all in a few
			clicks and a few minutes rather than the hours it used to take just to find where the degraded data
			sat. What nobody mandated was that the ETL team and the AI model team would come asking for the same
			logic, for pipeline runs and training data that looked nothing like ING's banking transactions. I had
			no authority over any of their roadmaps. <strong
				class="deck-stat"
				data-deck-label="One model"
				data-deck-foot="Seven teams, one lineage model|IBM watsonx"
				>Generalizing the model past banking data risked the ship date; staying narrow risked several teams
				quietly rebuilding the same thing.</strong
			> We generalized, and the original timeline held.
		</p>

		<ImagePair
			deckLabel="Before / after"
			items={[
				{
					fn: "Before",
					imgSrc: ibm11,
					imgAlt: "IBM Infosphere Information Governance Catalog, Data Lineage: an inventory panel listing 281 assets on the left and a dense diagram of hundreds of tiny nodes joined by grey lines, too small to read",
					fit: "contain",
					caption: "Hours to load, a hundred clicks to find the break, and nothing on screen to say which node was the problem.",
				},
				{
					fn: "After",
					imgSrc: ibm12,
					imgAlt: "IBM Watson Knowledge Catalog, Lineage tab for SAVINGS_ACCOUNTS: the starting asset sits in the centre with its business terms, three original-source files sit on the left folded to 2 of 24 assets, and two reports and a model marked Final target sit on the right",
					fit: "contain",
					caption: "Seconds to the first useful answer, and a path from finding the break to fixing it without leaving the screen.",
				},
			]}
		/>
	</section>

	<section id="outcome">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">04</span><h2>Outcome</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>Measured after launch, and in the years since:</p>
		<Metrics
			three
			items={[
				{ n: "−90%", label: "Loading time, at the high end. Time-to-value moved from hours to seconds" },
				{ n: "$100K+", label: "In new contracts, shaped by the three banks who'd co-designed the product" },
				{ n: "Red Dot", label: "Award for the redesign, with two products shipped at different pay tiers" },
			]}
		/>
		<p>
			The clearer measure is time. Six years after I left IBM, the same patterns are still part of the
			shipped lineage product, the ETL and AI model teams' designers adapted the model rather than building
			their own version of "trace where this came from," and the platform-wide backlog the journey work
			surfaced has since been built out by other designers. That's the evidence the abstraction landed at
			the right altitude: general enough to reuse, narrow enough to ship on time.
		</p>
		<Pull
			quote="I've never met such a well-rounded designer who is skilled in everything: UX design, producing pixel-perfect hi-fidelity designs, leading workshops, conducting user research independently, defining product requirements, and stakeholder management."
			attribution="Lauren Chen, co-worker at IBM"
			deckLabel="Testimonial"
		/>
		<Figure
			alt="IBM Cloud Pak for Data, Lineage tab for the gdpr-analysis-workbook report: opens on two original-source assets feeding a starting asset, then opens the Show query items panel to pick which columns appear in the lineage graph"
			figNum="Fig. 06"
			lead="Still the front door, six years later."
			tall
			fit="contain"
			deck
			deckLabel="Six years on"
			placeholderLabel="Figure 06"
		>
			The work outlived the team that built it. Open IBM's lineage today and it still opens folded to
			source and consumer, still expands a step at a time, and still asks which columns are worth showing
			before it draws them.
		</Figure>
	</section>

	<section id="reflection">
		<div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">05</span><h2>Reflection</h2><span class="sec-rule"></span></div>
		<p>
			The hardest part of this project wasn't the diagram. It was keeping seven teams aligned on decisions
			that had already been made. Meetings kept circling back to settled questions, and while some of that
			revisiting was healthy, I spent hours I didn't have re-litigating choices with stakeholders who'd
			agreed to them a month earlier. Alignment, it turned out, has a half-life.
		</p>
		<p>
			Because of this project I started keeping a decision diary: a running record, shared with
			stakeholders, of each decision, when it was made, and why. Starting it on day one would have given
			back most of those hours, and on a project where I was designing for durability under deadline
			pressure, knowing the backlog would outlive my own time at IBM, those hours mattered more than usual.
			Every project since has had one from the first week.
		</p>
	</section>

	<NextCaseStudy href="/case-studies/hpe-ai-troubleshooting-agent" label="Debugging's hardest step was asking for help" />
</CaseStudyLayout>
